 "note": "RPCS3 Core 928 = Core 927 plus the GPU gate closed when the application is deactivated (REWINDBOX_GPU_DEACTIVATION_GATE_928). Core 927 closed its gate at the background notification; the journal of the only device loss (IPA 650, pid 8767) has \"Device lost\" 30 ms before the host's own observer of that notification ran: the command buffer had been refused before the application handled it. Apple's Metal documentation (Preparing your Metal app to run in the background) gives the procedure the Core now follows: stop sending work when the application is deactivated, make sure what was committed is scheduled when it enters the background, enable the code again once it is reactivated. The gate closes and settles at UIApplicationWillResignActiveNotification; the background notification only finishes what is left (a resumed settle gives the queues nothing new); a renderer created while the application is not active closes the gate at once; the freeze episode sampler counts the closed time as paused; COREPROF GPUGATE928 replaces COREPROF GPUGATE927. While the application is inactive the picture stands still. Core 927 (pinned 6669475f, run 42946503173: asynchronous MoltenVK submits definitive for every title, the background gate, texture lookup memo served for Black Ops II, GPU memory packing, the deferral to a starving PPU before the line lock), unchanged otherwise. No codegen input changes: PPU and SPU codegen identities unchanged. No reservation skipped or weakened, no resolution, precision, frame limit, SPU count or priority or LLVM option changes. MoltenVK dependency of run 42957981240 (library d19d3a42, byte-identical to the one Core 914 to 927 linked; requested again because the dependency workflow is itself a recorded input and changed with the artifact upload protection). Built by the RPCS3 Core route (run 42960159566, attempt 1, source 6e6bbeee; Apple host harnesses run 42960159566 on 6e6bbeee). Verified offline on the downloaded artifacts with the repository verifier (pin_errors and bundle). Not measured on the iPhone; no FPS claim.",
